        "By the early 1960s three European Communities existed side by side: the European Coal and Steel Community, set up in 1952, and the European Economic Community and Euratom, both operating since 1958. They already shared two institutions. Under a convention signed together with the Rome treaties, a single parliamentary assembly and a single Court of Justice served all three. Each Community, however, still had its own executive and its own council of ministers: the coal and steel community was run by a High Authority, while the two newer Communities each had a Commission.\n\nThe treaty signed in Brussels on 8 April 1965, usually called the Merger Treaty, replaced the three executives with a single Commission and the three councils with a single Council. It entered into force on 1 July 1967, and the Belgian Jean Rey became the first President of the merged Commission. The treaty also gave a formal basis to the committee of national permanent representatives that prepares the Council's work.\n\nThe merger concerned the institutions, not the Communities themselves. The three remained distinct legal entities, each governed by its own founding treaty, so the single Commission applied different rules depending on which treaty it was acting under. The coal and steel community came to an end when its treaty expired in 2002, while Euratom still exists today alongside the European Union.",
