        "The Treaty on European Union, signed in Maastricht in February 1992, created the European Union and gave it an unusual structure, often described as a temple resting on three pillars. The first pillar consisted of the existing Communities, with the European Economic Community renamed the European Community to reflect its wider scope. Here the established 'Community method' applied: the Commission had the right of initiative, the Council frequently decided by qualified majority, and the Court of Justice could enforce the rules.\n\nThe two new pillars worked differently. The second covered a common foreign and security policy, and the third covered cooperation in justice and home affairs, including asylum, immigration, and police and judicial cooperation. In both, decision-making was essentially intergovernmental: the Council generally acted by unanimity, the Commission either shared the right of initiative with Member States or had none, and the Parliament and the Court played only a limited role. Governments wanted to work together in these sensitive areas without handing them over to supranational institutions.\n\nRatification was not smooth. Danish voters rejected the treaty in June 1992 and approved it in a second referendum in May 1993, after Denmark had obtained several opt-outs, and the treaty entered into force on 1 November 1993. Later revisions moved parts of the third pillar into the Community framework, before the pillar structure was abolished altogether in 2009.",
